    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d620">

                <pb facs="0692" n="1370" sameAs="frus:frus1863p2/pg1370"/>

                <head>Mr. Chase to Mr. Seward.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Treasury Department</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-10-28"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 28, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have the honor of acknowledging the
                    receipt of your communication of the 24th instant, relative to duties upon
                    articles furnished to foreign consuls in this country by their own governments
                    for official use.</p>
                <p>Your suggestion, “whether it may not be advisable that books, stationery, and
                    like articles, imported by foreign consuls resident in the United States, or
                    sent to them by their respective governments, and intended solely and entirely
                    for official use in their several consulates, be admitted free of duty,” is
                    worthy of consideration; and I will take early occasion to bring the subject to
                    the attention of Congress.</p>
                <p>With great respect, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S. P. CHASE, <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of the
                        Treasury.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. W. H. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Seward</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Secretary of State.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
